i didn't  think that sounded very dignified but it  ended up on the call sheet and it just  stuck who are you  before the release of hellraiser no one  realized how popular the guy with nails  in his face would end up being the hell  priest wasn't even the original poster  boy for the film pinhead actor doug  bradley told horroronline.com that a  flayed frank was originally the image  intended to grace posters and lunchboxes  at some point during production the  powers that be opted for the grid-faced  man to push the film into the darkest  corners of the public consciousness  bradley explained the image is so  original so startling it is just an  incredible image to look at and that  made a big difference in terms of the  public's perception of the movie  on one level hellraiser is a cautionary  tale about what happens to a curious  individual when he meddles in the  supernatural in another sense if you  look past the flesh flaying the screams  the carnage and the sadist with pins in  his face you'll find what clive barker  has referred to as a love story from  beyond the grave it might sound absurd  but take a closer look and you'll see  that barker isn't wrong the author  explained that just as its desire that  leads frank cotton to a lonely place  where he's skinned alive it's the same  desire that leads his brother's wife  julia cotton down the road to ruin julia  attempts to resurrect her old lover by  butchering unsuspecting men so frank can  feed on their precious blood but as  barker points out she doesn't do it  because she's a psychopath she does it  for love barker stressed in the same  interview that he intended hellraiser to  be an intelligent and popular horror  movie that didn't exploit women or  manipulate sensibilities he told  monsterland there has been a general  move towards the infantile in horror  pictures recently a desire to soften the  blows i hope this time we're going to  get tough again let's be honest the  cenobites are a pretty eye-catching  bunch if pinhead and his posse strut it  into a party it would certainly cause a  stir their look is hellish and with all  the leather and boots it's reminiscent  of the weirdest shock rock group never  to play a gig you wouldn't want to bump  into these guys in a graveyard or an s m  club the latter of which according to  clive barker is the inspiration for the  cenobite's distinctive look barker told  the guardian that both the physical and  emotional makeup of the cenobytes was  inspired by s m clubs he had frequented  specifically an underground club in new  york called block 28. barker revealed  that cell block 28 had a hardcore s m  night where there was no drinking or  drugs just intense pain pleasure and a  whole lot of piercings he elaborated it  was the first time i ever saw people  pierced for fun it was the first time i  saw blood spilt  oh no tears please  it's a waste of good suffering barker  explained that the move to make the  cenobites act and dress a certain way  was met with positive reception from the  s m community saying i was validating a  lifestyle it was a celebration of the  beauty of these strange secret rituals  having only made a couple of short films  with a group of friends in liverpool as  a teenager hellraiser was clive barker's  first experience helming a major movie  production directing a feature film for  the first time was a daunting task as he  revealed in an interview i didn't know  the rules i didn't know who the sound  guy was i only knew who the camera  operator was because he was the guy  behind the camera i don't think that  finally hurt the movie may have actually  been useful because i sort of made it up  as i went along when barker was young he  and a group of friends grabbed an eight  millimeter camera and shot two shorts  salome and the forbidden inspired by  underground and experimental directors  like kenneth anger future hellraiser  collaborators doug bradley and pete  atkins were involved in the productions  parker reflected on his work by saying  they are technically extremely crude and  their storyline's obscure actually i  think it has a strange poetry about it  that's strange poetry as well as the  themes of packs rituals killers and  sexuality have a lot in common with  hellraiser as we know it today the  puzzle box in hellraiser with which  frank opens the doors to another  dimension is integral to the franchise's  overarching plot the peculiar-looking  device also known as the lament  configuration was described in the  hellbound heart as one of several in  existence that was created by frenchman  philip lamarchon one is said to be  safely under lock and key in the  basement of the vatican another was  rumored to have been in the possession  of marquis de sade during his tenure at  the bastille and a third found its way  into the hands of an englishman named  frank  no don't touch it  it's dangerous  it opens doors  the inspiration for the puzzle box came  from an unlikely source clive barker's  grandfather according to an interview  with the podcast the geek's guide to the  galaxy barker wanted his characters to  access hell in a unique way he was  eventually drawn to a childhood memory  of an elaborate wooden puzzle box his  grandfather who was a ship's cook  brought back from the far east barker  recalled playing with the box a lot as a  child when he was puzzling about how  best his character in the hell-bound  heart could open the doors of hell he  remembered his grandfather's box and the  final piece fell into place  it's no good sweating buckets to create  a horror film designed to shock and  unnerve the audience if you pay little  attention to the soundtrack music adds  ambience atmosphere suspense and dread  to a movie in short the cenobytes can't  exactly share screen time with a disney  style soundtrack it would be terribly  unbecoming before hellraiser's release  industrial band coil delivered a  sonically charged hellscape of a  soundtrack to accompany clive barker's  film however it never made the final cut  and was replaced by christopher young's  more traditional score barker was  friendly with the members of coyle and  felt their unique sound would make a  perfect fit for hellraiser the studio  disagreed apparently deciding that the  soundtrack was just too unsettling  anyone familiar with coyle's work during  that period wouldn't have been surprised  as barker himself once said coil is the  only group i've heard on disc whose  records i've taken off because they make  my bowels churn however the truth as to  why coil's hellraiser soundtrack was  never given the green light is far more  pedestrian coils steven thrower told the  quietus that when barker asked the  producers for more special effects  funding they said something along the  lines of we didn't think this was going  to be as commercial as it is reshoot  those effect scenes and hear some more  money one stipulation we want our men to  do the score  movie sets are hectic places everyone's  got a job to do and everyone's rushing  around frantically trying not to get in  one another's way costume designers  camera operators production managers and  boom operators all play their part in a  constant effort to keep things from  going off the rails on the set of  hellraiser they also had their very own  maggot and cockroach wrangler to keep  things in order it was an odd job but a  necessary one in a pre-cgi age where  everyone had to get their hands dirty to  create something authentic in an  interview with film threat clive barker  pointed out that being on a movie set  for 14 straight hours and dealing with  30 different people all with equally  important jobs to do was far more tiring  than he had anticipated but relief came  with the insects barker enthused the  moments which were really exciting were  the major cockroach scenes which i  really liked we had a roach wrangler we  had tupperware you know the ultimate  tupperware container your roach  tupperware container in the fridge  barker explained that special passports  and visas were needed to import extra  large american cockroaches to the uk the  crew could only import male cockroaches  because there was a danger that they  could escape and procreate barker joked  somewhere in customs there's presumably  a roach sexer  although many people praised hellraiser  upon its release for breathing new life  into the horror genre not everyone was  won over by clive barker's vision famed  film critic roger ebert gave hellraiser  a paltry half star review decrying it as  a dreary sham he wrote somewhat  viciously this is one of those movies  you sit through with mounting dread as  the fear grows inside of you that it  will turn out to be feature length he  didn't care much for its sequel either  which he made clear on his tv show the  problem here is that there was no  discernible beginning middle or into  this nightmare just a series of  disconnected and gruesome scene if that  seems a tad harsh it gets worse ebert  questioned the motives of the audience  for going to see the movie in the first  place in his opinion hellraiser  contained no surprises or emotional  engagement and lacked any wit or flare  interestingly ebert's harsh verdict on  the film appeared to be the opening shot  in a war of words between the legendary  critic and clive barker as chronicled on  ebert's own website years after the  release of hellraiser barker would slam  the critic for making the controversial  statement that video games can never be  art parker described ebert as prejudiced  calling him a quote pompous arrogant old  man how much bearing ebert's notorious  review of hellraiser had on barker's  scathing words we'll never know but just  as criticism is always subjective an  artist never forgets a bad review  imagine pinhead and michael myers  battling it out in a cinematic crossover  along the lines of freddy vs jason or  alien vs predator and then imagine it  some more because it's never going to  happen however it nearly did although it  probably wouldn't have contained a lot  of small talk between the chief cenobite  and the masked murderer hellraiser vs  halloween was a promising premise  nevertheless paul kane's book the  hellraiser films and their legacy  reveals that filmmaker dave parker once  pitched the idea of a crossover event to  dimension films parker needed a way to  get the two villains together for a  rumble so he hatched an origin story in  which myers is trick-or-treating as a  child and receives a mysterious box from  a stranger in black boots upon opening  it the lord of the dead possesses  meyer's body and goes on a killing spree  since he's possessed by a demonic entity  myers cannot be killed until pinhead  shows up years later from there it's  game on dimension was excited enough to  reach out to both clive barker and john  carpenter and penciled in a release date  of october 31st 2004 however not only  did the owners of the halloween movie  rights throw shade on the idea a poll on  the official halloween website suggested  that the majority of fans didn't want  the movie to happen so it didn't  one of the most unsettling things about  the cenobytes apart from their  insatiable desire to inflict pain is  their unnerving silence unwavering eye  contact with unreadable yet intense  facial expressions combined with the art  of keeping your entire body extremely  still is the embodiment of psychological  intimidation the cenobites are obvious  masters at it with the exception of  pinhead who's not exactly mr talkative  the rest of the cenobites and hellraiser  are a pretty mute bunch even the  chatterer isn't much of a chatterbox in  total pinhead is only in the original  film for eight minutes and doesn't have  a lot of lines that's why it's no wonder  that combined with the industrial makeup  doug bradley thought he would be  unrecognizable in character in an  interview with the guardian bradley  explained that he was offered both the  role of pinhead and the bit part of a  moving man he had reservations about  playing a heavily made up monster saying  because hellraiser was my first movie i  thought it might be a good idea to be on  screen as myself but it was a brief  conversation there was no doubt in my  mind that this unnamed guy with the pins  in his head had a certain genesecua that  a mattress delivery guy didn't the first  time bradley saw himself in the mirror  as pinhead he knew he had made the right  decision he continued i had a sense of  majesty his threat is implied look what  i did to myself now imagine what i can  do to you  trick us again child  and your suffering will be legendary  even in hell  [Music]

